# The overworld's broadleaf, standing on a tree tile inside the playable area.
#
# Y is up, +Z is north, one world unit is one map tile. The lowest vertex sits
# on y = 0 and the model is centred on x = 0 and z = 0, so the game places it
# by its feet.
#
# Faces are wound so that (c - a) x (b - a) points out of the solid, which is
# what tools/obj2cpp.py bakes as the face normal. The ring convention is
# pine.obj's: (r cos t, y, r sin t) with the angle increasing, the upper half
# wound apex then ring[i] then ring[i+1], the lower half the other way round
# because its apex is underneath.
#
# A six sided bipyramid on a four sided trunk: exactly 20 triangles, the same
# budget as treepine.obj, 2.30 units tall. Round enough to read as a
# broadleaf against a conifer at eight pixels, and no rounder, because at
# this camera every extra ring is triangles spent below the resolution of the
# screen.
#
# A zone grows one species, never a mixture: `trees` in the .zone file picks
# between this and treepine.obj, and the sprites on that zone's border are
# drawn from the matching run of frames. A route that mixed the two read as
# two areas stitched together.

mtllib treeleaf.mtl

# the crown: top apex, the widest ring, then the bottom apex
v 0.000 2.300 0.000
v 0.680 1.450 0.000
v 0.340 1.450 0.589
v -0.340 1.450 0.589
v -0.680 1.450 0.000
v -0.340 1.450 -0.589
v 0.340 1.450 -0.589
v 0.000 0.900 0.000

# the trunk, top ring then bottom ring
v 0.120 1.000 0.000
v 0.000 1.000 0.120
v -0.120 1.000 0.000
v -0.000 1.000 -0.120
v 0.140 0.000 0.000
v 0.000 0.000 0.140
v -0.140 0.000 0.000
v -0.000 0.000 -0.140

usemtl leaf_canopy
f 1 2 3
f 1 3 4
f 1 4 5
f 1 5 6
f 1 6 7
f 1 7 2
f 8 3 2
f 8 4 3
f 8 5 4
f 8 6 5
f 8 7 6
f 8 2 7

usemtl leaf_bark
f 9 13 14 10
f 10 14 15 11
f 11 15 16 12
f 12 16 13 9
